														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4276056">
									

<p>Something interesting I thought of. If you flip &quot;Tails, this flip comes up heads.&quot;, You make a prediction only on the &quot;Tails&quot; side. So having the coin land on &quot;heads&quot; wouldn't create a contradiction.</p>
<p>Assuming The coin cannot effect on which side it falls (Or it always lands with the Tails side), then the contradiction would have been resolved like in the article.</p>
<p>But if the coin can effect its own flip, it should have landed on &quot;heads&quot; and keep its original property. (assuming the anomaly takes the path of least resistance)</p>
<p>So perhaps we can conclude one of two options:</p>
<p>1. The coin cannot effect its own flip.<br />
2. The coin always lands with the same side up. Regardless on what's on it or the predictions made.</p>

